      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hackberry got its start in 1875 when prospector Jim Music helped develop the Hackberry Silver Mine.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hackberry%2C_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from a hackberry tree beside a spring that marked the mine’s location.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hackberry%2C_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1883 the Atlantic & Pacific Railroad built a station through Hackberry, naming it after the nearby mine.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.theroute-66.com/hackberry.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 1920s Union 76 service station operated by John Grigg served Route 66 travelers until 1967.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hackberry%2C_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Northside Grocery, opened in 1934, and its Conoco station were among the last Route 66 businesses to close in 1978.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hackberry%2C_Arizona"
    },
    {
